# Plain Prometheus vs Thanos — Architecture Comparison

## 1. Architecture Diagram (Side by Side)

**Simple / Plain Prometheus**
```
Targets → scrape → Prometheus (single node)
                        │
                    memory (head) + WAL
                        │
                    2h blocks on LOCAL disk
                        │
                    Grafana queries Prometheus directly
```

**Prometheus + Thanos**
```
Targets → scrape → Prometheus (N nodes/clusters)
                        │
                memory (head) + WAL  ── same as before, unchanged
                        │
                    2h blocks on LOCAL disk (short retention 6–12h)
                        │
                  ┌─────┴─────┐
              Sidecar        (local disk stays short-lived)
                  │
              uploads to S3 (object storage) ── long-term storage
                  │
          Thanos Compactor (compacts/downsamples in S3)
                  │
          Thanos Store Gateway (serves S3 data back)
                  │
          Thanos Querier ← merges Sidecar (live) + Store Gateway (historical)
                  │
              Grafana queries Thanos Querier (ONE endpoint, ALL clusters)
```

**Key point:** Thanos doesn't replace Prometheus's scrape/WAL/head-block/2h-block pipeline — that part is **identical in both**. Thanos only adds components *after* the 2h block is created.

---

## 2. WAL — Same in Both

| | Simple Prometheus | Thanos |
|---|---|---|
| WAL exists? | Yes | Yes — unchanged, Thanos doesn't touch WAL |
| Purpose | Crash recovery for in-memory head block | Same — WAL is purely a Prometheus-internal mechanism |
| Where | Local disk only | Local disk only (Thanos never sees WAL, only finished blocks) |

So WAL is a **non-difference** — it's identical because Thanos sits downstream of it, only picking up completed immutable blocks.

