## 3. Full Comparison Table

| Aspect | Simple Prometheus | Prometheus + Thanos |
|---|---|---|
| **Nodes** | Single node, no clustering | Multiple Prometheus instances (per cluster/region), unified centrally |
| **Storage location** | Local disk only | Local disk (short-term) + S3/GCS object storage (long-term) |
| **Retention** | Can be long (days/weeks) but limited by disk size | Local kept short (6–12h); long-term lives in S3 (effectively unlimited) |
| **Scaling** | Vertical only (bigger CPU/RAM on one box) | Horizontal — add more Prometheus + Thanos scales storage/query independently |
| **Global view (multi-cluster)** | Not possible — each Prometheus is isolated | Yes — Thanos Querier gives one query across all clusters |
| **High availability** | Manual (run 2 Prometheus, no dedup) | Built-in dedup of HA-paired Prometheus replicas via Querier |
| **Compaction** | Local compactor merges blocks automatically | Local compaction **disabled**; Thanos Compactor handles it in S3 (avoids race condition) |
| **Downsampling** | Not available | Yes — 5m/1h resolution blocks for fast long-range queries |
| **Query path (live/recent data)** | Grafana → Prometheus directly | Grafana → Thanos Querier → Sidecar → Prometheus (proxied) |
| **Query path (historical data)** | Grafana → Prometheus (if data still on local disk) | Grafana → Thanos Querier → Store Gateway → S3 |
| **Cost** | Cheap for small setups, disk-bound | Object storage (S3) is cheap for large volume/long retention |
| **Operational complexity** | Low — one binary | Higher — Sidecar, Compactor, Store Gateway, Querier all need managing |
| **Failure/data-loss risk** | Node dies → lose whatever wasn't queried/exported | Node dies → data already shipped to S3 is safe; only unshipped (<2h) window at risk |

---

## 4. Where "Live Communication" Actually Happens

| Communication | In Simple Prometheus | In Thanos |
|---|---|---|
| Scrape → target | Prometheus scrapes target directly (HTTP pull) | Same — unchanged |
| Query → data | Grafana talks to Prometheus's `/api/v1/query` | Grafana talks to **Thanos Querier's** `/api/v1/query` (same API shape, so Grafana config barely changes — just swap the URL) |
| Recent/unshipped data | N/A (it's just local Prometheus) | Querier → **Sidecar** → Prometheus's own local API (live proxy, real-time) |
| Old/historical data | N/A | Querier → **Store Gateway** → range-GET requests to S3 |
| Block shipping | N/A | Sidecar → S3 (upload, one-way, batch every 2h) |
| Compaction | Prometheus's internal compactor, in-process | Thanos Compactor, separate process, runs against S3 only |

---

## 5. When to Use Which

| Situation | Use |
|---|---|
| Single small app/cluster, short retention is fine, no multi-cluster need | **Plain Prometheus** — simpler, fewer moving parts |
| Need retention beyond what local disk can hold (months/years) | **Thanos** |
| Multiple Kubernetes clusters / regions, want ONE dashboard/query view | **Thanos** |
| Need HA Prometheus pairs without duplicate data in graphs | **Thanos** (dedup in Querier) |
| Cost-sensitive long-term storage | **Thanos** (S3 >> disk for large volume) |
| Small team, want minimal ops overhead | **Plain Prometheus** |
| Compliance/DR — data must survive node loss | **Thanos** (data is durable in S3, not tied to one node's disk) |

---

## 6. One-Line Mental Model

> **Plain Prometheus** = scrape + store + query, all on one box, data dies with the disk.
> **Thanos** = keeps Prometheus's scrape/WAL/2h-block pipeline exactly as-is, then adds a *second life* for that data (S3) and a *single query window* (Querier) across as many Prometheus instances as you run.
